.TH "CRUFT" "1" "September 2026" "cruft" "cruft User Manual"
.SH NAME
.PP
cruft \- Calculinux reduced footprint framebuffer terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.B cruft
.RB [ command ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B cruft
is a Linux framebuffer terminal emulator derived from yaft, tuned for
low memory on Calculinux devices.
.PP
The alternate screen buffer (DECALT) is implemented, so full-screen
programs (vim, htop, less) run on their own buffer and leave the main
screen intact when they exit.
.PP
When a status font is installed, cruft draws a status bar in the margin
strip above the cell grid: the window title set by the foreground
program on the left, the clock in the centre, and a wifi signal stair
and battery indicator on the right. The title is the standard terminal
window title (escape sequence OSC 0/2, the same channel xterm-style
terminal emulators show in their title bars), so shells, editors and
other programs announce the current file or command; an empty title
clears it. Non-ASCII characters are dropped (the 4x8 status font has
no such coverage), and the title is trimmed so it never overlaps the
centred clock. A segment whose data source is absent (bare hardware,
VM) is hidden; a missing or unsuitable status font disables the bar
entirely and the grid reclaims the full screen.
.PP
Lines that scroll off the top of the main screen are kept in a
scrollback buffer (see
.BR CRUFT_HISTORY ): the newest rows in RAM, and older ones in a
session file when the requested depth exceeds the RAM tier (
.BR CRUFT_SB_RAM ).
While any history is stored, a thin scrollbar in the right edge of the
grid shows the current position; full-screen programs run on the
alternate screen and never pollute the history.
.PP
Images are shown through two protocols that both land on the same
sparse per-cell pixmaps, so scrolling, erasing and alt-screen behaviour
is identical whichever one a client picked:
.BR sixel
(device control sequence 62 t) and the
.B kitty graphics protocol
(APC \fIESC _ G\fP; the real kitty wire protocol as specified by
kovidgoyal/kitty, which ratatui-image speaks). Legacy iTerm2
.B OSC 1337
input is consumed and ignored without a reply. Decoded images are capped
at 512x512 pixels, anchored at the cursor, shrunk (aspect-preserving) to
fit the remaining grid, and never scroll the screen.
.PP
The kitty graphics path speaks the full transfer surface: chunked
uploads (\fIm=\fP), the image store with virtual placement (\fIU=1\fP),
re-placement (\fIa=p\fP), deletion (\fIa=d\fP), queries, and the
\fIi=31\fP capability probe. Display uses the protocol's Unicode
placeholder mechanism: the renderer prints U+10EEEE cells carrying the
image id in a truecolor foreground, and cruft paints the stored image
when the placeholder anchor arrives. Capability probes let the client
side detect all of this instead of falling back to text art: DA1
advertises the sixel bit (\fIESC [ ?62;4;6c\fP), \fICSI 16t\fP
reports the cell pixel size (height first), and DSR/CPR are answered.
Truecolor SGR colours render as the nearest palette colour, so an
existing cell keeps its colour when a later SGR changes the active one;
the exact value is kept only for the life of the colour, so image ids
riding a truecolor SGR decode unquantized.
zlib-compressed and shared-memory kitty payloads are declined politely.
.PP
Raw pixel transfers are always accepted; PNG (\fIf=100\fP) transfers
additionally need the binary built against the system libpng (detected
automatically; force the decision with
.B HAVE_PNG=0
or
.BR HAVE_PNG=1 ).
.PP
.SH KEYBOARD
.PP
History navigation:
.RS
.TP
\fBPrior\fP (\fBESC [5~\fP)
half a page deeper into the history
.TP
\fBNext\fP (\fBESC [6~\fP)
half a page towards the live tail
.TP
\fBCtrl+Home\fP (\fBESC [1;5H\fP)
the oldest stored row
.TP
\fBCtrl+End\fP (\fBESC [1;5F\fP)
the live tail
.RE
.PP
The page keys are native: on the PicoCalc the keyboard driver maps
Shift+Up / Shift+Down to them. The Ctrl+Home / Ctrl+End sequences come
from the shipped console keymap (picocalc.map); both halves of those
pairs are free on this keyboard, whose Home / End are themselves the
shifted Tab / Delete. Bare arrows, and bare Home / End, stay the
program's. On the main screen cruft intercepts the four sequences
before the foreground program sees them; full-screen programs run on
the alternate screen and own every key.
.PP
Any other key while browsing scrolls back to the live tail and is
delivered to the program. Sequences are matched only at the start of a
keypress, so a split-delivery ESC will occasionally pass through.
.SH CUSTOMIZATION
.PP
Rewrite conf.h before make.
The default status bar segment selection (used when
.B CRUFT_SB
is unset) is the
.B sb_default_segments
conf.h variable (default "all").
.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.TP
.B FRAMEBUFFER
Specify framebuffer device (default /dev/fb0).
.TP
.B CRUFT_FONT
Path to a CRUFTFN1 font blob (default /usr/share/cruft/console.cruftfont).
.TP
.B CRUFT
If this variable includes "wall", cruft enables wallpaper mode and a
framebuffer shadow buffer. See cruft_wall.
.TP
.B CRUFT_SB
Status bar segments: a comma-separated list of
.B time ,
.B wifi ,
.B battery
and
.BR title ,
or
.B all
(the default) to show every segment whose source is present,
.B none
(or
.BR 0 )
to disable the bar. Unknown names are ignored with a warning.
.TP
.B CRUFT_HISTORY
Total scrollback depth in rows, across the RAM tier and the disk
overflow (default 96, capped at 16384).
0 disables history and the scrollbar. Rows deeper than the RAM tier
(default 512, see
.BR CRUFT_SB_RAM )
spill to a session file in /var/tmp (created 0600, removed on exit);
a depth at or below the RAM tier keeps history fully in memory.
Stored rows carry copied cell contents; sixel pixmaps are kept per
row up to a 32 KiB budget on disk (a row that is almost all art
degrades to blank text) and in the RAM ring up to a 64 KiB total
budget, oldest art degrading to blank beyond it. A file that becomes
unreadable (crash, damage) silently disables the disk tier for the
session; the RAM tier and the screen keep working.
.TP
.B CRUFT_SB_RAM
Rows of scrollback kept in the RAM tier (default 512, 1 to 16384).
Only depths above this value in
.B CRUFT_HISTORY
are stored on disk.
.TP
.B CRUFT_SB_12H
Set to 1 for a 12-hour clock (\fIH:MMam/pm\fP); the default is
24-hour.
.TP
.B CRUFT_SB_FONT
Path to the 4x8 CRUFTFN1 status font
(default /usr/share/cruft/status.cruftfont, installed by the
miniwi-console package).
.SH CONFIGURATION
.PP
Persistent defaults can be set in
.B /etc/cruft.conf ,
one \fIKEY=VALUE\fP per line (\fB#\fP comments, blank lines and
whitespace around the \fB=\fP are ignored). The recognised keys are the
six \fICRUFT_*\fP variables above:
.BR CRUFT_FONT ,
.BR CRUFT_SB ,
.BR CRUFT_SB_FONT ,
.BR CRUFT_SB_12H ,
.BR CRUFT_HISTORY
and
.B CRUFT_SB_RAM .
.B FRAMEBUFFER
and
.B CRUFT
are environment-only and cannot be set in the file. The built-in
default applies when neither the file nor the environment sets a key.
Precedence: environment (per-service \fBEnvironment=\fP, exported shell
variable) overrides the file, which overrides the built-in. Unknown
keys and malformed values are skipped with a warning, as is a
.B CRUFT_FONT
value the file supplies that is not a loadable font (the built-in font
stands); a missing or empty file changes nothing.
